"Places subgroup bars side by side to compare values within and across categories.\n\nUse when: Each category contains the same small set of comparable subgroups. Readers need absolute subgroup magnitudes rather than proportions alone.\n\nAvoid when: The number of subgroups makes individual bars too narrow to compare.\n\nVisual grammar: Outer positions encode primary categories and inner positions encode subgroups. Bar length encodes values; consistent color identifies each subgroup.\n\nBuild: Create the full category-by-subgroup table and preserve absent combinations. Allocate equal subgroup slots within every category band. Draw all bars from zero and repeat subgroup order consistently.\n\nCheck: Check bars represent the same reporting period and units. Verify subgroup colors and positions agree across categories. Distinguish absent observations from actual zero totals.\n\nPitfalls: Inconsistent subgroup ordering forces repeated legend lookup. Dense clusters are better replaced by small multiple bar charts.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic weekday and weekend visits at five venues with two neighboring bars per venue.\n\nAlternatives: Bar chart, Stacked bar chart, Dot plot."
